1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:07,880
Listen.

2
00:00:08,520 --> 00:00:09,040
Practice.

3
00:00:09,560 --> 00:00:09,900
Speak.

4
00:00:10,610 --> 00:00:12,250
Practice makes you perfect.

5
00:00:13,930 --> 00:00:19,740
Today, we are plunging into a topic that's become pretty much universal, hasn't it?

6
00:00:20,200 --> 00:00:24,640
The glorious and sometimes, let's be honest, maddening world of video calls.

7
00:00:25,100 --> 00:00:30,310
You know, that feeling you're all set to connect and boom, technology throws a digital wrench in the works.

8
00:00:30,290 --> 00:00:31,050
Absolutely.

9
00:00:31,070 --> 00:00:31,730
Oh, yeah.

10
00:00:31,910 --> 00:00:35,840
Whether it's for work or catching up with friends, maybe even teaching remotely.

11
00:00:35,900 --> 00:00:38,360
We've all hit those spots.

12
00:00:38,860 --> 00:00:42,230
The picture freezes, sound cuts out, someone just vanishes.

13
00:00:42,890 --> 00:00:44,290
It's frustrating, definitely.

14
00:00:44,310 --> 00:00:49,480
And then figuring out how to actually talk about it in English, well, that can just add another layer of awkwardness.

15
00:00:49,460 --> 00:00:50,040
Exactly.

16
00:00:50,140 --> 00:00:55,870
Because just pointing and saying, your computer's broken, isn't quite going to cut it, is it?

17
00:00:56,050 --> 00:00:56,960
Not really, no.

18
00:00:56,980 --> 00:01:01,310
It's a really interesting look at the actual words and phrases native English speakers use.

19
00:01:02,130 --> 00:01:06,100
People in the U.S., the U.K., how they navigate these little tech troubles.

20
00:01:06,080 --> 00:01:09,920
Think of it like getting the real, you know, the on the ground language toolkit.

21
00:01:09,940 --> 00:01:20,270
We're basically here to equip you with the vocabulary, the phrasing, so you can confidently say things like you're breaking up or I think you might be on mute without missing a beat.

22
00:01:20,450 --> 00:01:20,720
Right.

23
00:01:21,140 --> 00:01:26,320
Our mission, make you sound like a pro even when the tech is, well, not cooperating.

24
00:01:26,480 --> 00:01:27,140
Okay, let's do it.

25
00:01:27,160 --> 00:01:27,640
Let's dive in.

26
00:01:27,760 --> 00:01:27,920
Right.

27
00:01:28,080 --> 00:01:28,300
All right.

28
00:01:28,320 --> 00:01:32,790
Let's start with maybe the most basic, but yeah, probably the most common problem.

29
00:01:32,870 --> 00:01:33,650
Yeah.

30
00:01:33,670 --> 00:01:34,050
Sound.

31
00:01:34,930 --> 00:01:35,530
Or...

32
00:01:35,510 --> 00:01:42,520
the lack of sound, that classic opening line of pretty much every video call seems to be... You can hear me?

33
00:01:42,880 --> 00:01:43,060
Right.

34
00:01:43,080 --> 00:01:43,380
Yeah.

35
00:01:43,660 --> 00:01:44,480
And it's interesting, isn't it?

36
00:01:44,500 --> 00:01:45,420
We always say hear.

37
00:01:45,480 --> 00:01:47,100
We don't really say listen.

38
00:01:47,220 --> 00:01:47,840
Oh, that's true.

39
00:01:48,080 --> 00:01:50,000
Yeah, our source material points out that distinction.

40
00:01:50,450 --> 00:01:52,950
Hear is about the physical ability, you know?

41
00:01:53,450 --> 00:01:56,150
The sound waves actually reaching your ears, your equipment working.

42
00:01:56,550 --> 00:01:58,970
Listen implies you're choosing to pay attention.

43
00:01:59,440 --> 00:01:59,810
Oh, okay.

44
00:01:59,830 --> 00:02:01,440
So when you ask, can you hear me?

45
00:02:01,880 --> 00:02:02,380
Mm-hmm.

46
00:02:02,540 --> 00:02:07,750
You're really just checking if the tech is working well enough for the sound to just arrive.

47
00:02:07,870 --> 00:02:08,630
That makes total sense.

48
00:02:08,650 --> 00:02:09,530
Just the basic function.

49
00:02:10,010 --> 00:02:13,030
So if the sound isn't arriving, what's the simplest way to say it?

50
00:02:13,910 --> 00:02:16,180
Probably just, sorry, I can't hear you at all.

51
00:02:16,220 --> 00:02:17,100
It's direct.

52
00:02:17,120 --> 00:02:18,300
There's no ambiguity there.

53
00:02:18,680 --> 00:02:19,200
Okay.

54
00:02:19,220 --> 00:02:22,970
And what about that little icon, the microphone with a line through it?

55
00:02:23,090 --> 00:02:23,310
Yeah.

56
00:02:23,320 --> 00:02:25,030
That seems to be the number one culprit, right?

57
00:02:25,210 --> 00:02:25,750
Oh, absolutely.

58
00:02:26,190 --> 00:02:27,910
That means someone is on mute.

59
00:02:27,890 --> 00:02:32,120
It's like a setting they've activated or maybe the software did it.

60
00:02:32,560 --> 00:02:35,090
So you'll often hear people say, it says you're on mute.

61
00:02:35,190 --> 00:02:36,860
Maybe I think your mic might be turned off.

62
00:02:37,220 --> 00:02:37,900
Turned off.

63
00:02:38,710 --> 00:02:40,150
That's just another way to say muted.

64
00:02:40,190 --> 00:02:41,650
Yeah, effectively, yes.

65
00:02:41,710 --> 00:02:44,030
And to fix it, you need to turn mute off.

66
00:02:44,120 --> 00:02:45,660
The state itself is on mute.

67
00:02:45,820 --> 00:02:46,320
Gotcha.

68
00:02:46,340 --> 00:02:49,460
We also use the adjective form like, I think your mic is muted.

69
00:02:49,520 --> 00:02:56,560
That feels maybe a bit more passive, suggesting the state it's in rather than saying they actively turned it off.

70
00:02:56,630 --> 00:03:00,780
And even softer is, you might be muted, you know, if you're not totally sure.

71
00:03:00,760 --> 00:03:01,940
Just giving them a little nudge.

72
00:03:02,290 --> 00:03:04,650
And how do you politely prompt them to actually check it?

73
00:03:04,790 --> 00:03:07,700
Well, phrases like, can you check if your mic is muted?

74
00:03:07,740 --> 00:03:08,940
Work well.

75
00:03:09,520 --> 00:03:12,010
Or a bit more generally, could you check your settings?

76
00:03:12,810 --> 00:03:16,120
That implies, you know, look at the options inside the program.

77
00:03:16,160 --> 00:03:16,460
Right.

78
00:03:16,440 --> 00:03:18,580
We saw this example scenario.

79
00:03:18,600 --> 00:03:19,540
It's so common.

80
00:03:19,860 --> 00:03:20,580
Call starts.

81
00:03:21,140 --> 00:03:21,970
Someone can't hear.

82
00:03:22,030 --> 00:03:24,190
They suggest, are you muted?

83
00:03:24,210 --> 00:03:26,230
The person checks.

84
00:03:26,450 --> 00:03:27,270
Oh, yep.

85
00:03:27,290 --> 00:03:27,790
Mic was off.

86
00:03:27,830 --> 00:03:28,450
They fix it.

87
00:03:28,990 --> 00:03:30,570
And then the call can actually begin.

88
00:03:30,610 --> 00:03:31,690
It's like the first hurdle.

89
00:03:31,850 --> 00:03:32,160
Yeah.

90
00:03:32,180 --> 00:03:32,800
Happens all the time.

91
00:03:32,940 --> 00:03:33,260
Yeah.

92
00:03:33,280 --> 00:03:36,020
So, OK, we've covered not being able to hear someone.

93
00:03:36,580 --> 00:03:38,920
What about when you can see them moving?

94
00:03:39,240 --> 00:03:42,030
They're clearly talking, but silence.

95
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:43,570
The classic combination.

96
00:03:43,730 --> 00:03:45,470
I can see you, but I can't hear you.

97
00:03:46,080 --> 00:03:49,530
It's really useful because it instantly narrows down the problem, doesn't it?

98
00:03:49,590 --> 00:03:50,750
It's not their camera.

99
00:03:50,770 --> 00:03:52,000
It's not the whole video link.

100
00:03:52,290 --> 00:03:54,880
It's specifically something with their audio set up.

101
00:03:55,000 --> 00:03:57,020
Okay, sound problems sorted, hopefully.

102
00:03:57,080 --> 00:03:57,320
Yeah.

103
00:03:57,560 --> 00:04:00,570
But often the issues are bigger than just the microphone, right?

104
00:04:00,590 --> 00:04:02,570
They involve the whole connection between everyone.

105
00:04:02,650 --> 00:04:05,870
This is where things can get really shaky, literally sometimes.

106
00:04:06,180 --> 00:04:11,940
And instead of saying something like, your internet is bad, which can feel a bit, well, confrontational.

107
00:04:11,960 --> 00:04:12,750
A bit pointed, yeah.

108
00:04:13,100 --> 00:04:13,610
Exactly.

109
00:04:13,650 --> 00:04:15,910
Native speakers often talk about the connection.

110
00:04:15,990 --> 00:04:21,480
So saying, sorry, the connection is really bad, makes it sound more like a general problem with the link itself.

111
00:04:21,460 --> 00:04:22,860
Maybe it's affecting everyone.

112
00:04:22,880 --> 00:04:23,960
Maybe it's the platform.

113
00:04:24,760 --> 00:04:27,830
It avoids pointing fingers directly at one person's Wi-Fi.

114
00:04:28,250 --> 00:04:28,950
That's a good tip.

115
00:04:29,450 --> 00:04:32,230
And the language here gets wonderfully descriptive, I noticed.

116
00:04:32,590 --> 00:04:36,070
Like when someone looks and sounds like a glitching robot.

117
00:04:36,200 --> 00:04:36,580
Ah, yes.

118
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:38,140
They are choppy.

119
00:04:38,160 --> 00:04:38,840
Choppy, okay.

120
00:04:38,860 --> 00:04:41,620
Yeah, the audio and the video are just cutting in and out really fast.

121
00:04:41,680 --> 00:04:45,110
It's a great word for that specific kind of broken, stuttering connection.

122
00:04:45,130 --> 00:04:47,450
Like, you're sounding very choppy today.

123
00:04:47,480 --> 00:04:48,020
Okay.

124
00:04:48,040 --> 00:04:48,980
And another classic.

125
00:04:49,040 --> 00:04:52,230
You hear this one in movies all the time with bad phone lines.

126
00:04:52,250 --> 00:04:53,490
You're breaking up.

127
00:04:53,890 --> 00:04:54,490
Definitely.

128
00:04:54,510 --> 00:04:59,330
This means their audio or their video or both is dropping out intermittently.

129
00:04:59,410 --> 00:05:01,740
Like they're there, then gone, then back, then gone again.

130
00:05:01,780 --> 00:05:02,520
Right.

131
00:05:02,540 --> 00:05:05,240
And yeah, it's exactly what you'd say on an old crackly phone call.

132
00:05:05,460 --> 00:05:06,400
Sorry, you're breaking up.

133
00:05:06,460 --> 00:05:07,640
I didn't catch that last part.

134
00:05:07,980 --> 00:05:08,880
And the dreaded delay.

135
00:05:08,940 --> 00:05:09,950
You say something, wait, wait.

136
00:05:11,110 --> 00:05:11,750
Then they react.

137
00:05:12,110 --> 00:05:13,150
Oh, that's lagging.

138
00:05:13,870 --> 00:05:18,700
You'd say it's lagging quite a bit or maybe just there seems to be a delay.

139
00:05:18,740 --> 00:05:21,840
It's that frustrating gap isn't it between the input and the output.

140
00:05:22,430 --> 00:05:22,530
Yeah.

141
00:05:22,550 --> 00:05:28,050
Lagging happens when the data just isn't traveling fast enough over the connection for whatever reason.

142
00:05:28,420 --> 00:05:39,110
Now it's interesting while we sort of avoid blaming their Internet directly our source material pointed out it's totally fine even kind of expected to blame your own Internet.

143
00:05:39,310 --> 00:05:40,130
Oh absolutely.

144
00:05:40,430 --> 00:05:43,820
We seem much more comfortable in meeting our own tech woes.

145
00:05:44,360 --> 00:05:46,930
You might say, sorry, my Internet has been acting up.

146
00:05:47,290 --> 00:05:48,590
That phrase acting up is great.

147
00:05:48,650 --> 00:05:53,540
It just means misbehaving, not working the way it should, like a, you know, a naughty child or something.

148
00:05:53,560 --> 00:05:54,840
Yeah, like it has a mind of its own.

149
00:05:54,940 --> 00:05:55,360
Exactly.

150
00:05:55,620 --> 00:06:00,030
Or more simply, you could just say, my Internet has been a bit slow today.

151
00:06:00,050 --> 00:06:01,730
Totally acceptable.

152
00:06:01,830 --> 00:06:03,050
So, OK, the connection's bad.

153
00:06:03,770 --> 00:06:05,680
How do you suggest troubleshooting?

154
00:06:05,840 --> 00:06:07,520
What kind of phrases are useful there?

155
00:06:07,700 --> 00:06:11,810
Well, one common strategy is just trying to reset the link to the call itself.

156
00:06:11,890 --> 00:06:15,000
So let me try reconnecting or maybe let me rejoin.

157
00:06:15,040 --> 00:06:19,770
It just means you'll leave the virtual room and pop back in hoping for a cleaner connection.

158
00:06:19,750 --> 00:06:21,960
Like stepping out of the room and coming back in again.

159
00:06:21,980 --> 00:06:23,000
Exactly like that.

160
00:06:23,300 --> 00:06:26,260
And sometimes physical location actually matters too.

161
00:06:26,540 --> 00:06:26,720
Right.

162
00:06:26,760 --> 00:06:27,690
The Wi-Fi signal.

163
00:06:27,700 --> 00:06:28,020
Yeah.

164
00:06:28,650 --> 00:06:32,870
So a polite suggestion might be, could you maybe try moving closer to the router?

165
00:06:33,150 --> 00:06:35,360
You know, that little box blinking in the corner.

166
00:06:36,360 --> 00:06:36,480
Right.

167
00:06:36,500 --> 00:06:42,050
And a fun fact from the material, you'll often hear router pronounced differently.

168
00:06:42,370 --> 00:06:46,750
Maybe more like router in the U.S. versus rotor in the U.K.,

169
00:06:47,730 --> 00:06:48,670
Subtle differences.

170
00:06:49,310 --> 00:06:52,080
And sometimes a router itself just needs a kick.

171
00:06:52,340 --> 00:06:52,620
Right.

172
00:06:53,120 --> 00:06:54,880
The classic tech support fix.

173
00:06:55,200 --> 00:06:58,550
A polite way to suggest that is, do you think you could maybe reset your router?

174
00:06:59,110 --> 00:06:59,210
Yeah.

175
00:06:59,230 --> 00:07:02,310
This usually means, you know, unplugging it, waiting a bit, plugging it back in.

176
00:07:02,370 --> 00:07:03,870
Standard procedure.

177
00:07:04,110 --> 00:07:08,660
And what about checking if something else on their computer is secretly using up all the bandwidth?

178
00:07:09,640 --> 00:07:10,060
Ah, yeah.

179
00:07:10,080 --> 00:07:10,380
Good one.

180
00:07:10,620 --> 00:07:13,270
You could ask, is there possibly something running in the background?

181
00:07:13,350 --> 00:07:20,940
This refers to other programs, maybe updates downloading automatically, that might be quietly hogging the connection.

182
00:07:20,980 --> 00:07:25,250
We saw an example dialogue where someone's connection was really choppy.

183
00:07:25,910 --> 00:07:30,720
And it turned out it was because a huge file was downloading silently in the background.

184
00:07:31,320 --> 00:07:32,910
It's surprisingly common.

185
00:07:33,020 --> 00:07:33,740
I could believe that.

186
00:07:33,840 --> 00:07:34,080
Okay.

187
00:07:34,100 --> 00:07:37,970
So we know how to talk about sound issues, connection problems.

188
00:07:38,670 --> 00:07:40,930
What about when the picture itself is the problem?

189
00:07:41,270 --> 00:07:43,270
Webcam stuff, camera troubles.

190
00:07:43,390 --> 00:07:43,620
Right.

191
00:07:43,770 --> 00:07:49,320
So generally we tend to use webcam for computers and maybe camera more for phones.

192
00:07:49,360 --> 00:07:52,990
Although camera can definitely work for built in laptop cameras too.

193
00:07:53,350 --> 00:07:54,810
It's pretty interchangeable sometimes.

194
00:07:55,070 --> 00:07:55,390
Okay.

195
00:07:55,410 --> 00:07:57,490
The basic checks are kind of similar to sound.

196
00:07:57,630 --> 00:08:00,950
Is your video on or is your video connected properly?

197
00:08:00,940 --> 00:08:04,360
And just like you might say, I can't hear you, you might just simply say, I can't see you.

198
00:08:04,600 --> 00:08:05,060
Exactly.

199
00:08:05,200 --> 00:08:10,090
And the material clarifies this doesn't mean you're like physically not looking at the screen.

200
00:08:10,550 --> 00:08:13,430
It means their video feed isn't actually appearing on your screen.

201
00:08:13,590 --> 00:08:13,910
Got it.

202
00:08:14,370 --> 00:08:17,320
What if they were there and then they just vanished from view?

203
00:08:17,760 --> 00:08:19,920
An informal way to ask is just, oh, where'd you go?

204
00:08:20,200 --> 00:08:20,460
Okay.

205
00:08:20,700 --> 00:08:25,470
Or if their little video window is just empty black, you'd say, it's just a black screen for me.

206
00:08:25,730 --> 00:08:27,390
And you might follow up with, are you still there?

207
00:08:27,650 --> 00:08:30,450
Just to check if they've dropped off the call entirely.

208
00:08:30,470 --> 00:08:32,930
And the universal sign of a really bad connection.

209
00:08:33,090 --> 00:08:33,370
Yeah.

210
00:08:33,390 --> 00:08:34,170
The frozen picture.

211
00:08:34,750 --> 00:08:35,130
Oh, yes.

212
00:08:35,810 --> 00:08:36,830
You're frozen.

213
00:08:37,290 --> 00:08:39,120
Your video feed just stops moving.

214
00:08:39,220 --> 00:08:39,660
Right.

215
00:08:39,680 --> 00:08:46,410
Often, as the material hilariously points out, mid gesture or with a really awkward facial expression.

216
00:08:46,430 --> 00:08:46,770
Always.

217
00:08:47,090 --> 00:08:47,390
Always.

218
00:08:47,830 --> 00:08:53,010
You might also say, maybe less commonly, you're not moving at all.

219
00:08:53,130 --> 00:08:55,970
OK. And the inverse of the earlier sound problem.

220
00:08:56,600 --> 00:08:58,840
I can hear you fine, but I can't see you.

221
00:08:59,680 --> 00:09:03,140
And just like the sound version, it tells them exactly what part isn't working.

222
00:09:03,650 --> 00:09:05,070
We saw a quick example of this, too.

223
00:09:05,310 --> 00:09:06,490
Someone says, hey, you're frozen.

224
00:09:06,530 --> 00:09:08,330
The other person says, OK, let me try reconnecting.

225
00:09:08,370 --> 00:09:12,040
They disappear for a second, then reappear moving normally again.

226
00:09:12,380 --> 00:09:13,020
Problem solved.

227
00:09:13,160 --> 00:09:15,810
OK. Now, sometimes it's more than just a glitch, isn't it?

228
00:09:15,870 --> 00:09:17,970
It's like a complete disconnection.

229
00:09:17,990 --> 00:09:18,090
Yeah.

230
00:09:18,110 --> 00:09:19,270
They're just gone from the call.

231
00:09:19,250 --> 00:09:19,650
Right.

232
00:09:19,910 --> 00:09:23,340
So you might say, oh, I think I lost you or you just disappeared.

233
00:09:24,520 --> 00:09:28,310
These are pretty common ways to describe someone suddenly leaving the call interface.

234
00:09:28,590 --> 00:09:32,740
And what if the whole call just ends unexpectedly for everyone?

235
00:09:32,920 --> 00:09:36,720
You might say it just cut off or you can use it as a phrasal verb.

236
00:09:36,920 --> 00:09:38,210
The call cut us off.

237
00:09:38,290 --> 00:09:40,050
Ah, it cut us off.

238
00:09:40,370 --> 00:09:43,660
That kind of implies the software or the connection itself was the problem, right?

239
00:09:43,780 --> 00:09:44,880
Not really anyone's fault.

240
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:45,520
Exactly.

241
00:09:45,540 --> 00:09:54,150
It often suggests a problem with the platform, maybe the server, or just the general link, rather than pointing to one specific person's equipment failing.

242
00:09:54,600 --> 00:09:54,700
Okay.

243
00:09:55,040 --> 00:10:01,950
And if the actual internet service itself stops working, we use phrases like the internet drops or the call dropped.

244
00:10:01,930 --> 00:10:05,430
That word drop implies the connection was lost completely.

245
00:10:05,590 --> 00:10:09,490
And when they finally managed to get back into the call, what do they usually say?

246
00:10:09,570 --> 00:10:12,170
Often something simple and effective like, hi, I'm back.

247
00:10:12,640 --> 00:10:14,700
Or maybe acknowledging the disruption.

248
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:15,800
Sorry about that.

249
00:10:15,860 --> 00:10:16,780
I don't know what happened there.

250
00:10:17,640 --> 00:10:25,570
Sometimes the material suggests it's just easier to move on after a quick drop off rather than trying to diagnose it right in the middle of the conversation.

251
00:10:25,690 --> 00:10:27,270
Yeah, probably best sometimes.

252
00:10:27,770 --> 00:10:30,010
And what if you know the problem was definitely on your side?

253
00:10:30,090 --> 00:10:33,070
How do you sort of take responsibility for it?

254
00:10:33,430 --> 00:10:37,800
A very useful phrase here is it was something on my end.

255
00:10:37,980 --> 00:10:38,660
On my end.

256
00:10:38,820 --> 00:10:39,480
Okay.

257
00:10:39,590 --> 00:10:51,050
This directly contrasts with blaming the connection generally, saying on my end clearly signals the problem originated with your setup, your Internet, your computer, your software, whatever.

