505
00:22:02,070 --> 00:22:05,850
Okay, so we know how to talk about sound issues, connection problems.

506
00:22:06,430 --> 00:22:07,450
What about when the

507
00:22:07,430 --> 00:22:11,660
Picture itself is the problem, webcam stuff, camera troubles.

508
00:22:11,940 --> 00:22:12,160
Right.

509
00:22:12,360 --> 00:22:21,640
So generally we tend to use webcam for computers and maybe camera more for phones, although camera can definitely work for built-in laptop cameras too.

510
00:22:22,280 --> 00:22:23,680
It's pretty interchangeable sometimes.

511
00:22:23,760 --> 00:22:24,360
Okay.

512
00:22:24,380 --> 00:22:26,300
The basic checks are kind of similar to sound.

513
00:22:26,660 --> 00:22:29,630
Is your video on or is your video connected properly?

514
00:22:29,610 --> 00:22:33,650
And just like you might say, I can't hear you, you might just simply say, I can't see you.

515
00:22:33,670 --> 00:22:34,320
Exactly.

516
00:22:34,340 --> 00:22:34,720
Yeah.

517
00:22:34,740 --> 00:22:39,580
And the material clarifies this doesn't mean you're like physically not looking at the screen.

518
00:22:39,880 --> 00:22:40,120
Right.

519
00:22:40,240 --> 00:22:42,910
It means their video feed isn't actually appearing on your screen.

520
00:22:42,950 --> 00:22:43,330
Got it.

521
00:22:43,690 --> 00:22:47,430
What if they were there and then they just vanish from view?

522
00:22:47,800 --> 00:22:49,800
An informal way to ask is just, oh, where'd you go?

523
00:22:50,120 --> 00:22:50,420
Okay.

524
00:22:50,560 --> 00:22:53,200
Or if their little video window is just empty, black.

525
00:22:54,040 --> 00:22:55,810
You'd say, it's just a black screen for me.

526
00:22:56,330 --> 00:22:58,610
And you might follow up with, are you still there?

527
00:22:59,570 --> 00:23:01,780
Just to check if they've dropped off the call entirely.

528
00:23:02,140 --> 00:23:06,500
And the universal sign of a really bad connection, the frozen picture.

529
00:23:06,620 --> 00:23:06,920
Oh, yes.

530
00:23:07,440 --> 00:23:08,030
You're frozen.

531
00:23:08,770 --> 00:23:10,270
Your video feed just stops moving.

532
00:23:10,450 --> 00:23:10,710
Right.

533
00:23:10,990 --> 00:23:17,440
Often, as the material hilariously points out, mid-gesture or with a really awkward facial expression.

534
00:23:17,540 --> 00:23:17,840
Always.

535
00:23:18,440 --> 00:23:21,910
You might also say, maybe less commonly, you're not moving at all.

536
00:23:21,920 --> 00:23:24,810
Okay, and the inverse of the earlier sound problem.

537
00:23:25,190 --> 00:23:28,440
I can hear you fine, but I can't see you.

538
00:23:29,160 --> 00:23:32,330
And just like the sound version, it tells them exactly what part isn't working.

539
00:23:32,670 --> 00:23:33,950
We saw a quick example of this, too.

540
00:23:34,410 --> 00:23:35,430
Someone says, hey, you're frozen.

541
00:23:35,470 --> 00:23:37,370
The other person says, okay, let me try reconnecting.

542
00:23:37,820 --> 00:23:41,080
They disappear for a second, then reappear, moving normally again.

543
00:23:41,420 --> 00:23:42,060
Problem solved.

544
00:23:42,220 --> 00:23:42,820
Okay.

545
00:23:42,840 --> 00:23:44,660
Now, sometimes it's more than just a glitch, isn't it?

546
00:23:44,720 --> 00:23:46,410
It's like a complete disconnection.

547
00:23:46,430 --> 00:23:47,510
Yeah, they're just gone from the call.

548
00:23:48,110 --> 00:23:48,330
Right.

549
00:23:48,370 --> 00:23:50,150
So you might say, oh, I think I lost you.

550
00:23:50,840 --> 00:23:52,960
Or you just disappeared.

551
00:23:53,440 --> 00:23:57,430
These are pretty common ways to describe someone suddenly leaving the call interface.

552
00:23:57,670 --> 00:24:01,680
And what if the whole call just ends unexpectedly for everyone?

553
00:24:01,720 --> 00:24:03,570
You might say, it just cut off.

554
00:24:04,650 --> 00:24:07,450
Or you can use it as a phrasal verb, the call cut us off.

555
00:24:07,540 --> 00:24:08,780
Ah, it cut us off.

556
00:24:09,100 --> 00:24:12,960
That kind of implies the software or the connection itself was the problem, right?

557
00:24:12,980 --> 00:24:14,290
Not really anyone's fault.

558
00:24:14,410 --> 00:24:14,870
Exactly.

559
00:24:15,290 --> 00:24:23,660
It often suggests a problem with the platform, maybe the server or just the general link, rather than pointing to one specific person's equipment failing.

560
00:24:23,920 --> 00:24:24,500
Okay.

561
00:24:24,520 --> 00:24:30,670
And if the actual internet service itself stops working, we use phrases like the internet drops or the call dropped.

562
00:24:30,690 --> 00:24:33,780
That word drop implies the connection was lost completely.

563
00:24:33,980 --> 00:24:37,520
And when they finally manage to get back into the call, what do they usually say?

564
00:24:37,860 --> 00:24:40,930
Often something simple and effective like, hi, I'm back.

565
00:24:40,950 --> 00:24:43,830
Or maybe acknowledging the disruption.

566
00:24:44,190 --> 00:24:44,780
Sorry about that.

567
00:24:44,800 --> 00:24:45,720
I don't know what happened there.

568
00:24:46,660 --> 00:24:52,850
Sometimes the material suggests it's just easier to move on after a quick drop off rather than trying to diagnose it right in the middle of conversation.

569
00:24:53,080 --> 00:24:54,800
Yeah, probably best sometimes.

570
00:24:55,640 --> 00:24:58,670
And what if you know the problem was definitely on your side?

571
00:24:59,170 --> 00:25:01,690
How do you sort of take responsibility for it?

572
00:25:02,130 --> 00:25:05,580
Ah, a very useful phrase here is, it was something on my end.

573
00:25:05,840 --> 00:25:07,000
On my end, okay.

574
00:25:07,020 --> 00:25:07,180
Yeah.

575
00:25:07,620 --> 00:25:11,010
This directly contrasts with blaming the connection, generally.

576
00:25:11,390 --> 00:25:18,820
Saying, on my end, clearly signals the problem originated with your setup, your internet, your computer, your software, whatever.

577
00:25:19,000 --> 00:25:19,660
Right.

578
00:25:19,640 --> 00:25:21,790
We saw a little example dialogue for this, too.

579
00:25:22,230 --> 00:25:24,710
Someone disappeared, came back saying, I'm back.

580
00:25:25,070 --> 00:25:26,210
The other person asked what happened.

581
00:25:26,230 --> 00:25:27,210
They said, I just cut off.

582
00:25:27,590 --> 00:25:30,240
And the first person just replied, oh, no big deal.

583
00:25:30,260 --> 00:25:31,380
And they just moved on.

584
00:25:31,400 --> 00:25:33,260
Perfect illustration of recovering gracefully.

585
00:25:33,480 --> 00:25:36,470
OK, so we've got all this vocabulary now to identify the problems.

586
00:25:36,510 --> 00:25:43,660
But how do you suggest these solutions to someone else politely, you know, without sounding like you're bossing them around or blaming them?

587
00:25:43,770 --> 00:25:45,210
Yeah, the framing is everything here.

588
00:25:45,820 --> 00:25:48,800
The material highlights some really helpful opening phrases.

589
00:25:49,340 --> 00:25:51,220
Things like maybe check.

590
00:25:52,080 --> 00:25:52,700
Could you try?

591
00:25:52,740 --> 00:25:54,390
What about?

592
00:25:54,430 --> 00:25:56,550
Or can you see if?

593
00:25:56,770 --> 00:25:57,130
Hold on.

594
00:25:57,170 --> 00:25:57,830
Can you see if?

595
00:25:58,310 --> 00:26:01,360
That one sounds a bit unusual if you're not used to it.

596
00:26:01,480 --> 00:26:02,560
Like see what?

597
00:26:02,610 --> 00:26:04,570
It does sound a bit odd out of context.

598
00:26:04,590 --> 00:26:07,980
But as the material explains, in this situation, can you see if?

599
00:26:08,000 --> 00:26:10,830
It doesn't mean physically looking to see something.

600
00:26:11,170 --> 00:26:15,350
It means can you check if or can you find out if or can you determine if?

601
00:26:15,650 --> 00:26:16,510
Ah, got it.

602
00:26:16,530 --> 00:26:16,770
Okay.

603
00:26:16,950 --> 00:26:18,490
Like, can you see if your mic is plugged in?

604
00:26:18,530 --> 00:26:20,920
Actually means, could you please check if your mic is plugged in?

605
00:26:20,940 --> 00:26:21,380
Precisely.

606
00:26:21,520 --> 00:26:22,020
Yeah.

607
00:26:22,040 --> 00:26:25,580
Other examples from the source were things like, can you see if your mute button is on?

608
00:26:25,860 --> 00:26:28,060
And co, can you see if your webcam is connected properly?

609
00:26:28,720 --> 00:26:34,990
It's a really common and quite polite way to ask someone to investigate a specific setting or a physical connection.

610
00:26:35,070 --> 00:26:35,730
Okay, that's useful.

611
00:26:36,030 --> 00:26:40,040
What about suggesting actions like restarting the router or rejoining the call?

612
00:26:40,250 --> 00:26:42,440
You could use what about or how about.

613
00:26:42,600 --> 00:26:45,900
So what about reconnecting or how about restarting your computer?

614
00:26:45,980 --> 00:26:49,090
These are easy ways to just float an idea out there.

615
00:26:49,570 --> 00:26:50,110
Right.

616
00:26:50,130 --> 00:26:52,570
You can also use that structure with ing after try.

617
00:26:52,610 --> 00:26:56,560
Like, could you try checking your sound settings or could you try reconnecting?

618
00:26:56,960 --> 00:27:00,340
Using the gerund, the yinging form, is very typical.

619
00:27:00,440 --> 00:27:04,470
And using could instead of can, that makes it sound even softer, more polite.

620
00:27:04,630 --> 00:27:05,070
Exactly.

621
00:27:05,190 --> 00:27:07,330
Could you check your settings?

622
00:27:07,830 --> 00:27:11,060
It just implies possibility and adds that layer of politeness.

623
00:27:11,980 --> 00:27:21,670
As the material notes, if you're pretty sure the problem isn't on your end, using could really softens the suggestion when you ask them to check their end.

624
00:27:22,070 --> 00:27:24,760
Like, could you perhaps check your camera settings?

625
00:27:24,840 --> 00:27:28,000
Yeah, or could you maybe check your mic settings?

626
00:27:28,020 --> 00:27:28,600
Something like that.

627
00:27:28,620 --> 00:27:32,710
And what if you're just completely out of ideas, like the last resort?

628
00:27:32,930 --> 00:27:38,140
The sort of last dish suggestion often sounds something like, hmm, is there maybe something in your camera settings?

629
00:27:38,570 --> 00:27:40,790
Or is there something weird in your mic settings maybe?

630
00:27:40,830 --> 00:27:41,090
Okay.

631
00:27:41,070 --> 00:27:48,000
It kind of implies, look, I don't know what else it could be, but maybe there's some obscure setting buried in there causing the issue.

632
00:27:48,360 --> 00:27:52,270
We saw a final, longer example dialogue that really brought a lot of these together.

633
00:27:52,770 --> 00:27:53,530
Someone couldn't hear.

634
00:27:53,590 --> 00:27:57,600
They went through suggesting checking settings, maybe trying to reconnect, checking the mic.

635
00:27:58,020 --> 00:28:01,950
And finally, the person figured out, ah, wait, yep, it was something on my end.

636
00:28:02,210 --> 00:28:02,740
Wow.

637
00:28:03,040 --> 00:28:11,510
OK, this deep dive has definitely given us a seriously useful collection of phrases for those moments when technology just refuses to play ball.

638
00:28:11,810 --> 00:28:12,130
Yeah.

639
00:28:12,210 --> 00:28:14,410
From asking that most basic, can you hear me?

640
00:28:14,930 --> 00:28:20,440
All the way to diagnosing why someone is choppy or admitting, oops, sorry, that was something on my end.

641
00:28:20,860 --> 00:28:25,330
You hopefully now have the language that real English speakers actually use to handle these situations.

642
00:28:25,670 --> 00:28:28,910
Well, smoothly or at least more smoothly.

643
00:28:28,890 --> 00:28:29,460
Definitely.

