00:33:28,320 --> 00:33:31,080
Rather than pointing fingers at just one person's Wi-Fi.

751
00:33:31,090 --> 00:33:37,550
And the language here gets wonderfully descriptive, like when someone looks and sounds like a glitching robot.

752
00:33:38,610 --> 00:33:39,830
Ah, yes, they are choppy.

753
00:33:40,070 --> 00:33:40,650
That's the word.

754
00:33:40,830 --> 00:33:41,270
Choppy.

755
00:33:41,290 --> 00:33:41,710
Yeah.

756
00:33:41,730 --> 00:33:44,780
The audio and video are cutting in and out really fast.

757
00:33:44,820 --> 00:33:47,670
It's a great word for that specific kind of broken signal.

758
00:33:48,070 --> 00:33:49,690
You're very choppy today.

759
00:33:49,870 --> 00:33:51,040
And another classic one.

760
00:33:51,060 --> 00:33:53,020
You definitely hear this in movies sometime.

761
00:33:53,080 --> 00:33:53,840
You're breaking up.

762
00:33:54,620 --> 00:33:55,120
Yeah.

763
00:33:55,140 --> 00:33:55,620
Yeah.

764
00:33:55,640 --> 00:33:59,440
This means their audio or maybe their video is dropping out like intermittently.

765
00:33:59,530 --> 00:34:00,090
In and out.

766
00:34:00,110 --> 00:34:00,310
Yeah.

767
00:34:00,330 --> 00:34:00,710
They're there.

768
00:34:00,830 --> 00:34:01,250
Then gone.

769
00:34:01,270 --> 00:34:01,830
Then back.

770
00:34:01,850 --> 00:34:03,810
It's exactly what you'd say on a bad phone line, too.

771
00:34:03,850 --> 00:34:05,310
You know, sorry, you're breaking up.

772
00:34:05,350 --> 00:34:05,990
I can't quite hear.

773
00:34:06,050 --> 00:34:06,450
Right.

774
00:34:06,910 --> 00:34:08,100
And the dreaded delay.

775
00:34:08,140 --> 00:34:10,820
You say something and then there's that pause.

776
00:34:10,800 --> 00:34:11,500
That's lagging.

777
00:34:11,720 --> 00:34:12,180
Lagging.

778
00:34:12,300 --> 00:34:14,130
It's lagging quite a bit or there's a delay.

779
00:34:14,570 --> 00:34:17,840
It's that frustrating gap between, you know, input and output.

780
00:34:18,140 --> 00:34:21,250
Lagging happens when the data just isn't traveling fast enough.

781
00:34:21,310 --> 00:34:22,110
Now, it's interesting.

782
00:34:22,570 --> 00:34:30,050
While we try to avoid blaming their internet, our source material points out it's totally fine, even expected, to blame your own internet.

783
00:34:30,300 --> 00:34:30,970
Oh, absolutely.

784
00:34:31,390 --> 00:34:34,370
We seem much more comfortable admitting our own tech woes.

785
00:34:34,510 --> 00:34:34,750
Right.

786
00:34:35,070 --> 00:34:37,580
You might say, sorry, my internet has been acting up.

787
00:34:38,140 --> 00:34:40,120
That phrase, acting up, it's great.

788
00:34:40,680 --> 00:34:43,200
It just means misbehaving, not working properly.

789
00:34:43,460 --> 00:34:44,930
Like a moody teenager.

790
00:34:45,270 --> 00:34:45,830
Kind of.

791
00:34:46,510 --> 00:34:50,370
Or, you know, more simply, my internet has been a bit slow today.

792
00:34:50,790 --> 00:34:55,520
Okay, so how do you suggest troubleshooting when the connection is generally bad?

793
00:34:55,570 --> 00:34:57,280
What are some useful phrases?

794
00:34:57,390 --> 00:35:01,740
Well, one common strategy is just trying to reset the link to the call itself.

795
00:35:02,200 --> 00:35:02,800
How do you mean?

796
00:35:02,820 --> 00:35:05,400
Like saying, let me try reconnecting or let me rejoin.

797
00:35:05,580 --> 00:35:05,980
Yeah.

798
00:35:06,000 --> 00:35:10,850
It basically means you'll leave the virtual room and then come straight back in, hoping for a cleaner connection.

799
00:35:10,910 --> 00:35:12,550
Like popping out and back through the door.

800
00:35:12,770 --> 00:35:13,170
Exactly.

801
00:35:13,410 --> 00:35:16,350
Sometimes physical location matters too.

802
00:35:16,630 --> 00:35:21,680
So a polite suggestion might be, could you try moving closer to the router?

803
00:35:22,020 --> 00:35:23,820
That little box blinking in the corner.

804
00:35:23,990 --> 00:35:25,850
Yeah, the thing sending out the Wi-Fi.

805
00:35:26,370 --> 00:35:33,000
And fun fact, from the material, you'll often hear router pronounced differently, like router maybe in the U.S.

806
00:35:33,200 --> 00:35:34,760
Compared to rotor in the U.K.

807
00:35:34,840 --> 00:35:36,400
Right, subtle differences.

808
00:35:36,500 --> 00:35:39,720
And sometimes the router itself needs, well, a kick.

809
00:35:39,980 --> 00:35:41,760
Right, the classic tech support fix.

810
00:35:42,450 --> 00:35:46,330
A polite way to suggest it is, do you think you could reset your router?

811
00:35:46,430 --> 00:35:49,530
Which usually means unplugging it, waiting a bit, plugging it back in.

812
00:35:49,610 --> 00:35:51,490
Exactly, that standard procedure.

813
00:35:51,580 --> 00:35:56,100
And what about checking if something else on their computer is using up all the bandwidth?

814
00:35:56,440 --> 00:35:58,840
Yeah, you could ask, is there something running in the background?

815
00:35:59,260 --> 00:36:05,350
This could be, you know, other programs or maybe updates downloading that are quietly hogging the internet speed.

816
00:36:05,990 --> 00:36:06,810
Ah, okay.

817
00:36:06,830 --> 00:36:13,220
We saw an example dialogue where a choppy connection got fixed because someone realized, oh, a huge file was downloading behind the scenes.

818
00:36:13,550 --> 00:36:14,650
It's surprisingly common.

819
00:36:14,690 --> 00:36:14,990
Right.

820
00:36:15,650 --> 00:36:18,610
Okay, so we know how to talk about sound and connection issues.

821
00:36:19,130 --> 00:36:21,320
What about when the picture itself is the problem?

822
00:36:21,880 --> 00:36:23,540
Webcam or camera troubles?

823
00:36:23,610 --> 00:36:24,070
Yeah.

824
00:36:24,090 --> 00:36:31,710
Generally, we tend to say webcam for computers and camera for phones, although camera often works fine for built-in laptop ones too.

825
00:36:32,050 --> 00:36:32,470
Makes sense.

826
00:36:32,700 --> 00:36:34,460
The basic checks are pretty similar to sound.

827
00:36:34,620 --> 00:36:35,460
Is your video on?

828
00:36:36,360 --> 00:36:38,080
Or maybe, is your video connected?

829
00:36:38,260 --> 00:36:42,610
And just like you can't hear someone, you might just simply say, I can't see you.

830
00:36:43,050 --> 00:36:43,250
Right.

831
00:36:43,690 --> 00:36:47,410
And the material clarifies, this doesn't mean you're physically not looking at the screen.

832
00:36:47,870 --> 00:36:50,360
It means their video feed just isn't showing up for you.

833
00:36:50,790 --> 00:36:51,270
Okay.

834
00:36:51,590 --> 00:36:53,590
What if they just vanish completely from view?

835
00:36:53,810 --> 00:36:55,700
An informal way to ask is just, where'd you go?

836
00:36:56,200 --> 00:36:58,360
Or if their little video window is just empty.

837
00:36:58,560 --> 00:36:58,880
Blank.

838
00:36:59,020 --> 00:36:59,840
It's just a black screen.

839
00:36:59,880 --> 00:37:00,180
Yeah.

840
00:37:00,200 --> 00:37:02,740
And you might follow that up with, are you still there?

841
00:37:03,490 --> 00:37:05,590
Just to check if they've dropped off the call entirely.

842
00:37:05,730 --> 00:37:09,530
And the universal sign of a dodgy connection, the frozen picture.

843
00:37:09,550 --> 00:37:10,110
Oh, yes.

844
00:37:10,750 --> 00:37:11,330
You're frozen.

845
00:37:11,930 --> 00:37:13,720
Your video feed just stocks dead.

846
00:37:13,860 --> 00:37:14,000
Mm-hmm.

847
00:37:15,060 --> 00:37:20,310
Often, as the material hilariously points out, like mid-gesture or with a really weird expression.

848
00:37:20,550 --> 00:37:21,210
Always.

849
00:37:21,230 --> 00:37:24,900
You might also say, maybe less commonly, you're not moving at all.

850
00:37:25,340 --> 00:37:29,670
And the flip side of the sound problem, I can hear you, but I can't see you.

851
00:37:29,760 --> 00:37:30,340
Exactly.

852
00:37:30,900 --> 00:37:35,090
And just like the sound version, it tells them precisely what part isn't working.

853
00:37:36,490 --> 00:37:37,790
We saw a quick example of this.

854
00:37:38,310 --> 00:37:40,050
Someone says the other person is frozen.

855
00:37:40,430 --> 00:37:41,740
They say, okay, let me try reconnecting.

856
00:37:42,100 --> 00:37:45,440
They disappear, then pop back up, moving normally again.

857
00:37:45,970 --> 00:37:46,810
Problem solved.

858
00:37:46,830 --> 00:37:47,210
Okay.

859
00:37:47,190 --> 00:37:49,710
Now, sometimes it's more than just a glitch, right?

860
00:37:49,730 --> 00:37:51,320
It's a complete disconnection.

861
00:37:51,500 --> 00:37:53,640
Yeah, they just poof, gone from the call.

862
00:37:53,660 --> 00:37:54,470
How do you say that?

863
00:37:54,610 --> 00:37:55,630
You could say, oh, I lost you.

864
00:37:56,070 --> 00:37:57,250
Or maybe you just disappeared.

865
00:37:57,750 --> 00:38:01,800
Those are pretty common ways to describe someone suddenly leaving the call unexpectedly.

866
00:38:01,990 --> 00:38:05,350
And if the whole call just ends abruptly for everyone.

867
00:38:05,490 --> 00:38:07,510
You might say it just cut off.

868
00:38:07,530 --> 00:38:07,890
Right.

869
00:38:08,070 --> 00:38:09,300
Or it cut us off.

870
00:38:09,980 --> 00:38:14,820
That phrasing kind of implies it was the software or the connection itself, doesn't it?

871
00:38:14,840 --> 00:38:15,340
Exactly.

872
00:38:15,610 --> 00:38:21,990
It often suggests a problem with the platform or the general link rather than, you know, pointing to one person's setup.

873
00:38:22,270 --> 00:38:30,420
And if the actual Internet service stops working, we use phrases like the Internet drops or the call dropped.

874
00:38:30,400 --> 00:38:33,250
That word drop implies the connection was lost completely.

875
00:38:33,490 --> 00:38:36,130
And when they managed to claw their way back into the call.

876
00:38:36,370 --> 00:38:38,900
Usually something simple and effective like, hi, I'm back.

877
00:38:39,340 --> 00:38:40,800
Or maybe acknowledging the interruption.

878
00:38:40,860 --> 00:38:41,900
Sorry, I don't know what happened there.

879
00:38:42,000 --> 00:38:42,850
Yeah.

880
00:38:42,870 --> 00:38:51,180
And sometimes the material suggests it's just easier to move on after a brief drop off rather than getting bogged down trying to diagnose it mid-conversation.

881
00:38:51,310 --> 00:38:51,870
Good point.

882
00:38:51,930 --> 00:38:52,410
Just carry on.

883
00:38:52,610 --> 00:38:54,740
Now, what if you know the problem was on your side?

884
00:38:55,340 --> 00:38:58,400
How do you sort of take responsibility for the glitch?

885
00:38:58,420 --> 00:39:01,860
Ah, a very useful phrase here is, it was something on my end.

886
00:39:02,320 --> 00:39:03,110
On my end.

887
00:39:03,330 --> 00:39:03,570
Okay.

888
00:39:03,590 --> 00:39:13,640
Yeah, this directly contrasts with blaming the connection generally, saying on my end clearly signals the issue started with your setup, your internet, your computer, your software, whatever.

889
00:39:13,740 --> 00:39:14,620
Got it.

890
00:39:14,600 --> 00:39:17,790
We saw an example where someone disappeared, came back saying, I'm back.

891
00:39:18,430 --> 00:39:21,480
Maybe use it just cut off to explain.

892
00:39:21,780 --> 00:39:24,730
And the other person just said something like, oh, no worries or no big deal.

893
00:39:25,110 --> 00:39:25,930
And they just moved on.

894
00:39:26,600 --> 00:39:28,740
A perfect example of recovering gracefully.

895
00:39:28,890 --> 00:39:39,500
Okay, so we've got the vocabulary now to identify all these different problems, but how do you suggest solutions to someone else politely without sounding like you're, you know, bossing them around?

896
00:39:39,840 --> 00:39:41,480
Framing is absolutely key here.

897
00:39:41,680 --> 00:39:47,040
The material highlights some really helpful opening phrases, things like maybe check.

898
00:39:47,020 --> 00:39:47,840
Could you try?

899
00:39:47,880 --> 00:39:49,000
What about?

900
00:39:49,520 --> 00:39:51,460
Or can you see?

901
00:39:52,100 --> 00:39:53,070
Can you see if?

902
00:39:53,430 --> 00:39:55,350
That one sounds a little bit odd if you're not used to it.

903
00:39:55,370 --> 00:39:56,330
Like see if what?

904
00:39:56,570 --> 00:39:58,170
It does sound a bit strange out of context.

905
00:39:58,970 --> 00:40:03,100
But as the material explains, it doesn't mean physically looking to see something.

906
00:40:03,280 --> 00:40:09,330
In this situation, can you see if really means can you check if or can you find out if.

907
00:40:09,430 --> 00:40:10,410
Ah, okay.

908
00:40:10,550 --> 00:40:12,830
Like, can you see if your mic is plugged in?

909
00:40:12,850 --> 00:40:15,020
It means, could you check that your mic is plugged in?

910
00:40:15,140 --> 00:40:15,620
Precisely.

911
00:40:16,220 --> 00:40:19,100
Or other examples from the source, can you see if your mute button is on?

912
00:40:19,500 --> 00:40:21,430
Can you see if your webcam is connected?

913
00:40:22,230 --> 00:40:28,280
It's actually a really common and quite polite way to ask someone to investigate a specific setting or connection.

914
00:40:28,380 --> 00:40:29,340
OK, that's useful.

915
00:40:29,720 --> 00:40:33,760
What about suggesting actual actions like restarting something?

916
00:40:33,820 --> 00:40:36,410
What about reconnecting or how about restarting?

917
00:40:36,790 --> 00:40:39,190
Those are easy ways to just float an idea.

918
00:40:39,650 --> 00:40:42,970
You could also use that structure with Shiging after try.

919
00:40:42,990 --> 00:40:45,240
Like, could you try checking your sound?

920
00:40:45,560 --> 00:40:46,720
Could you try reconnecting?

921
00:40:47,320 --> 00:40:49,420
Using the gerund form there is very natural.

922
00:40:49,400 --> 00:40:53,470
And using could instead of can just makes it sound a bit softer, more polite, right?

923
00:40:53,490 --> 00:40:53,930
Exactly.

924
00:40:53,970 --> 00:40:55,370
Could you check your settings?

925
00:40:55,390 --> 00:40:58,240
It just implies possibility and adds that layer of politeness.

926
00:40:58,700 --> 00:41:06,270
As the material notes, if you're pretty sure the problem isn't on your side, using could definitely softens the suggestion.

927
00:41:06,310 --> 00:41:08,470
Like, could you perhaps check your camera settings?

928
00:41:08,550 --> 00:41:09,110
Exactly.

929
00:41:09,600 --> 00:41:11,040
Or your mic settings.

930
00:41:11,100 --> 00:41:12,480
And what if you're completely stumped?

931
00:41:12,500 --> 00:41:13,620
You've run out of ideas.

932
00:41:14,190 --> 00:41:19,500
Well, the sort of last-ditch suggestion often sounds like, is there something in your camera settings?

933
00:41:20,300 --> 00:41:22,320
Or is there something in your mic settings?

934
00:41:22,580 --> 00:41:23,280
Implying?

935
00:41:23,700 --> 00:41:30,270
It kind of implies, look, I don't know what it is, but maybe there's some obscure setting buried in there that's causing the problem.

936
00:41:30,370 --> 00:41:30,970
Have a look.

937
00:41:30,990 --> 00:41:31,320
Got it.

938
00:41:31,380 --> 00:41:32,340
The final resort.

939
00:41:32,540 --> 00:41:33,140
Yeah.

940
00:41:33,160 --> 00:41:37,660
We saw a final, longer example dialogue that actually pulled a lot of these together.

941
00:41:37,680 --> 00:41:38,420
Someone couldn't hear.

942
00:41:38,460 --> 00:41:42,030
They went through suggesting checking settings, trying to reconnect.

943
00:41:42,390 --> 00:41:42,530
Mm-hmm.

944
00:41:43,170 --> 00:41:46,110
And finally figured out, OK, it was something on my end.

945
00:41:46,910 --> 00:41:50,100
It showed that whole troubleshooting process using this kind of language.

946
00:41:50,280 --> 00:41:50,720
Wow.

947
00:41:51,180 --> 00:41:59,960
OK, this deep dive has definitely given us a seriously useful collection of phrases for those moments when technology just flat out refuses to cooperate.

948
00:41:59,940 --> 00:42:00,580
Absolutely.

949
00:42:00,840 --> 00:42:03,160
From asking the most basic, can you hear me?

950
00:42:03,950 --> 00:42:09,410
Right through to diagnosing why someone is choppy or having to admit, oops, it was something on my end.

951
00:42:09,950 --> 00:42:15,450
You now have the language that, you know, real English speakers use to handle these glitches smoothly.

952
00:42:15,760 --> 00:42:15,990
Yeah.

953
00:42:16,120 --> 00:42:19,360
Moving past those awkward silences and the robotic voices.

954
00:42:19,980 --> 00:42:21,560
It isn't just about fixing the tech, is it?

955
00:42:21,580 --> 00:42:24,340
It's about having the confidence to actually talk about the problem.

956
00:42:24,680 --> 00:42:25,420
Exactly.

957
00:42:25,520 --> 00:42:28,770
Hopefully this gives you that confidence for your next online chat or meeting.

958
00:42:28,910 --> 00:42:29,980
And it's interesting, isn't it?

959
00:42:30,320 --> 00:42:41,400
Think about how even these seemingly simple technical glitches, a frozen screen, a dropped call, they actually highlight how complex communication itself really is.

960
00:42:42,120 --> 00:42:42,600
Good point.

961
00:42:42,740 --> 00:42:49,290
The language we choose in those moments, like deciding between saying the connection is bad versus it was something on my end.

962
00:42:49,830 --> 00:42:51,170
Well, that's not just technical talk.

963
00:42:51,270 --> 00:42:53,560
It subtly navigates the social situation.

964
00:42:53,570 --> 00:42:55,250
Assigning responsibility or not.

965
00:42:55,270 --> 00:42:55,750
Exactly.

966
00:42:56,030 --> 00:42:59,060
Or framing it as a shared problem we need to solve together.

967
00:42:59,560 --> 00:43:03,550
How we talk about the tech failure actually changes the human interaction that's happening through the tech.

968
00:43:03,930 --> 00:43:07,490
Something to maybe consider next time your screen goes blank.
